 Given that \( x \equiv 4 \pmod{19} \) and \( y \equiv 7 \pmod{19} \). We need to find the remainder when \((x + 1)^2 (y + 5)^3\) is divided by 19. We can start by substituting \( x \) and \( y \) with their equivalents modulo 19 in the expression. So, we have: \[
x + 1 \equiv 4 + 1 \equiv 5 \pmod{19}
\] \[
y + 5 \equiv 7 + 5 \equiv 12 \pmod{19}
\] Now we need to find \((x + 1)^2 (y + 5)^3 \mod 19\), which is equivalent to \(5^2 \cdot 12^3 \mod 19\). We can calculate this step by step. First, we find \(5^2 \mod 19\): \[
5^2 = 25 \equiv 25 - 19 = 6 \pmod{19}
\] Next, we find \(12^3 \mod 19\): \[
12^2 = 144 \equiv 144 - 7 \cdot 19 = 144 - 133 = 11 \pmod{19}
\] \[
12^3 = 12 \cdot 12^2 \equiv 12 \cdot 11 = 132 \equiv 132 - 6 \cdot 19 = 132 - 114 = 18 \pmod{19}
\] Now we combine these results: \[
5^2 \cdot 12^3 \equiv 6 \cdot 18 = 108 \pmod{19}
\] We can simplify \(108 \mod 19\) as follows: \[
108 = 108 - 5 \cdot 19 = 108 - 95 = 13 \pmod{19}
\] So the remainder when \((x + 1)^2 (y + 5)^3\) is divided by 19 is 13. </think> <answer>13</answer>

